<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android is allowed to copy off this device. Nothing.

  SSHetu's database holds every host, tunnel, snippet and known-host pin the
  user has, and `flutter_secure_storage` keeps the passwords and private keys
  in EncryptedSharedPreferences whose master key lives in the AndroidKeyStore.
  Two things follow, and they point the same way:

  - **Privacy.** An app holding the keys to someone's production
    infrastructure has no business pushing a copy of that inventory to a
    cloud backup the user never asked for. The same reasoning as PROJECT.md
    §12, "No backend".
  - **Correctness.** The AndroidKeyStore master key is non-exportable, so a
    restored copy of the encrypted preferences is ciphertext nothing on the
    new device can open. Backing up the database without the secrets restores
    a host list where every credential silently fails — worse than restoring
    nothing.

  `android:allowBackup="false"` in the manifest already stops Google Drive
  backup. It does **not** stop the Android 12+ device-to-device transfer, so
  that is excluded here as well.

  Moving to a new device is a deliberate act in this app: Settings → Backup
  (an encrypted file, Argon2id + XSalsa20-Poly1305) or Send to a device (a
  one-shot sealed transfer over the local network). Both carry the secrets,
  and both happen because the user chose them.

  Guarded by test/android_manifest_test.dart.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
